window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["flask"]=[{"id":"flask-f0001","card":"q-flask-001-1-what-flask-is-and-the-project","sec":"flask-s002","q":"What is Flask, and what does \"micro framework\" mean?","a":"<p>Flask is a Python web framework with a small core: routing, request and response handling, Jinja templating and a development server (built on Werkzeug). \"Micro\" does not mean small apps only. It means Flask makes no choices about the database, form library or authentication, and you add those through extensions. The trade-off is flexibility and a gentle learning curve against having to pick and wire the pieces yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0002","card":"q-flask-001-1-what-flask-is-and-the-project","sec":"flask-s002","q":"What kinds of applications is Flask a good fit for?","a":"<p>Small to medium web apps, REST APIs, microservices, prototypes and MVPs, and dashboards or data-visualization apps. It is popular in data science because a model or a chart can be exposed with a few lines of Python.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0003","card":"q-flask-001-1-what-flask-is-and-the-project","sec":"flask-s002","q":"Which libraries does Flask build on, and what does each one do?","a":"<p>Werkzeug provides the WSGI toolkit (routing, request and response objects, the dev server). Jinja2 provides the template engine. Everything else, such as forms, ORM and login, comes from extensions like Flask-WTF, Flask-SQLAlchemy and Flask-Login.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0004","card":"q-flask-001-1-what-flask-is-and-the-project","sec":"flask-s002","q":"Why use a virtual environment for a Flask project, and how do you make one?","a":"<p>A virtual environment keeps each project's dependencies isolated, so two projects can use different versions of Flask without conflict. Create it with <code>python3 -m venv venv</code> (the <code>venv</code> module ships with Python 3.3+), activate it with <code>source venv/bin/activate</code> (macOS/Linux) or <code>venv\\Scripts\\activate</code> (Windows), then <code>pip install Flask</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0005","card":"q-flask-002-flask-vs-django","sec":"flask-s003","q":"How do Flask and Django differ in philosophy?","a":"<p>Flask is a micro framework: minimal core, you choose the structure and components. Django is a full-stack framework with an ORM, admin, authentication and migrations built in, and it follows convention over configuration. Flask gives more freedom and a gentler start. Django gives more out of the box and a consistent structure across a team.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0006","card":"q-flask-002-flask-vs-django","sec":"flask-s003","q":"When would you pick Flask over Django, and when the reverse?","a":"<p>Pick Flask for small or medium apps, APIs, microservices, prototypes and dashboards where you want control over each component. Pick Django for large apps that need an admin panel, authentication, an ORM and a CMS-style feature set quickly, or for teams that want enforced conventions. The trade-off is that with Flask you assemble and maintain more yourself, and with Django you accept its structure and a steeper learning curve.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0007","card":"q-flask-002-flask-vs-django","sec":"flask-s003","q":"A team needs a user-management admin interface and a relational model with migrations in two weeks. Which framework do you suggest and why?","a":"<p>Django. It ships an admin site, authentication and ORM migrations, so the work is configuration. With Flask you would assemble Flask-SQLAlchemy, Flask-Migrate, Flask-Login and an admin extension, which is doable but slower to set up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0008","card":"q-flask-004-2-environment-setup-and-first-app","sec":"flask-s005","q":"In this minimal Flask app, why is <code>__name__</code> passed to <code>Flask(...)</code>?","a":"<p><code>__name__</code> is the name of the current module. Flask uses it to decide the root path of the app, which is where it looks for the <code>templates</code> and <code>static</code> folders, and it also appears in logging and error messages.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import Flask\napp = Flask(__name__)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"flask-f0009","card":"q-flask-004-2-environment-setup-and-first-app","sec":"flask-s005","q":"In a Flask script, why is <code>app.run(debug=True)</code> wrapped in <code>if __name__ == \"__main__\":</code>?","a":"<p>The guard makes the dev server start only when the file is run directly (<code>python app.py</code>). If another module imports the file, such as a test, a WSGI server or <code>flask run</code>, the server must not start. Without the guard, importing the module would launch a second server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0010","card":"q-flask-004-2-environment-setup-and-first-app","sec":"flask-s005","q":"What does <code>debug=True</code> do in <code>app.run</code>, and why should it never be on in production?","a":"<p>It enables the auto-reloader, which restarts the server on code changes, and the interactive debugger, which shows tracebacks in the browser. The debugger allows arbitrary code execution from the browser, so exposing it publicly is a serious security hole. Production runs behind a WSGI server such as Gunicorn with debug off.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0011","card":"q-flask-004-2-environment-setup-and-first-app","sec":"flask-s005","q":"What is the difference between running <code>python app.py</code> and <code>flask run</code>?","a":"<p><code>python app.py</code> runs the <code>app.run(...)</code> call in your file and needs the <code>__main__</code> guard. <code>flask run</code> uses the Flask CLI, which finds the app through <code>FLASK_APP</code> (or <code>--app app</code> in Flask 2.2+), and takes options like <code>--debug</code>. The CLI approach also works with factory functions and is the recommended way in current Flask docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0012","card":"q-flask-004-2-environment-setup-and-first-app","sec":"flask-s005","q":"Which address and port does the Flask development server listen on by default?","a":"<p><code>127.0.0.1</code> (localhost only) on port 5000. Other machines cannot reach it unless you pass <code>host=\"0.0.0.0\"</code> or <code>--host</code>, which you should only do deliberately, since the dev server is not hardened.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0013","card":"q-flask-005-project-structure","sec":"flask-s006","q":"In a typical Flask project, what goes in the <code>templates</code> and <code>static</code> folders, and why those names?","a":"<p><code>templates/</code> holds Jinja HTML files used by <code>render_template</code>. <code>static/</code> holds files served as-is, such as CSS, JavaScript and images, available under the <code>/static/</code> URL. Flask looks for these two folder names relative to the app's root path by default, so no configuration is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0014","card":"q-flask-005-project-structure","sec":"flask-s006","q":"Why is it better to keep forms, models and routes in separate modules as a Flask app grows?","a":"<p>A single <code>app.py</code> becomes hard to read and test, and mixing everything encourages circular imports once models need <code>db</code> and routes need models. Separate modules (and later Blueprints and an application factory) keep each concern small and testable. For a tiny app, one file is fine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0015","card":"q-flask-006-routes-and-view-functions","sec":"flask-s007","q":"In Flask, what is the difference between a route and a view function?","a":"<p>A route is the URL rule registered with <code>@app.route(...)</code>. The view function is the Python function that runs when a request matches that rule and returns the response. The decorator links the two.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0016","card":"q-flask-006-routes-and-view-functions","sec":"flask-s007","q":"What can a Flask view function return?","a":"<p>A string (treated as HTML), a <code>render_template(...)</code> result, a <code>redirect(...)</code> response, a <code>jsonify(...)</code> JSON response, or a tuple like <code>(body, status)</code> or <code>(body, status, headers)</code>. Flask turns the return value into a <code>Response</code> object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0017","card":"q-flask-006-routes-and-view-functions","sec":"flask-s007","q":"Why use <code>render_template</code> instead of returning an HTML string from a view?","a":"<p>Templates separate presentation from Python logic, support inheritance and loops, and auto-escape variables to reduce XSS risk. Building HTML in strings with f-strings gets unreadable and can inject unescaped user input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0018","card":"q-flask-006-routes-and-view-functions","sec":"flask-s007","q":"What happens if two view functions in one Flask app have the same function name?","a":"<p>Flask uses the function name as the endpoint name by default, so the second registration raises an <code>AssertionError</code> about overwriting an existing endpoint function. Rename one function or pass an explicit <code>endpoint=</code> to the decorator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0019","card":"q-flask-007-url-parameters","sec":"flask-s008","q":"In Flask, what does a route like <code>@app.route(\"/post/&lt;int:post_id&gt;\")</code> do?","a":"<p>It captures that part of the URL as a variable, converts it to an <code>int</code>, and passes it to the view as the <code>post_id</code> argument. One rule then serves <code>/post/1</code>, <code>/post/2</code> and so on. If the segment is not an integer, Flask does not match the route and returns 404.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0020","card":"q-flask-007-url-parameters","sec":"flask-s008","q":"Which URL converters does Flask provide, and what does each accept?","a":"<p><code>string</code> (default) accepts text without slashes. <code>int</code> accepts positive integers. <code>float</code> accepts positive floating-point values. <code>path</code> is like string but also accepts slashes. There are also <code>uuid</code> and <code>any</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0021","card":"q-flask-007-url-parameters","sec":"flask-s008","q":"When would you use the <code>path</code> converter?","a":"<p>When the variable can contain slashes, for example <code>/files/&lt;path:filename&gt;</code> capturing <code>docs/2024/report.pdf</code>. The default <code>string</code> converter stops at the first slash.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0022","card":"q-flask-007-url-parameters","sec":"flask-s008","q":"In this route, why use <code>posts.get(post_id)</code> and return a 404 rather than <code>posts[post_id]</code>?","a":"<p><code>dict.get</code> returns <code>None</code> for a missing key, while <code>posts[post_id]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> and gives the client a 500 error. Returning 404 tells the client the resource does not exist. In real apps <code>abort(404)</code> achieves the same and triggers Flask's error handlers.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>posts = {1: {&quot;title&quot;: &quot;First&quot;}}\n\n@app.route(&quot;/post/&lt;int:post_id&gt;&quot;)\ndef show_post(post_id):\n    post = posts.get(post_id)\n    if not post:\n        return &quot;Not found&quot;, 404\n    return post[&quot;title&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"flask-f0023","card":"q-flask-007-url-parameters","sec":"flask-s008","q":"In Flask, what is the difference between a URL path parameter and a query-string parameter, and how do you read each?","a":"<p>A path parameter is part of the route (<code>/post/5</code>) and arrives as a view argument. A query-string parameter comes after <code>?</code> (<code>/search?q=flask</code>) and is read from <code>request.args.get(\"q\")</code>. Use path parameters to identify a resource and the query string for optional filters, sorting or search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0024","card":"q-flask-008-the-three-project-routes-app-py","sec":"flask-s009","q":"In Flask, why does a form route need <code>methods=[\"GET\", \"POST\"]</code>?","a":"<p>Routes accept only GET (and HEAD, OPTIONS) by default. A form page is fetched with GET and submitted with POST to the same URL, so both methods must be allowed. Inside the view, <code>request.method</code> or <code>form.validate_on_submit()</code> tells which case you are handling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0025","card":"q-flask-008-the-three-project-routes-app-py","sec":"flask-s009","q":"What is the POST-Redirect-GET pattern and why use it in Flask?","a":"<p>After handling a POST, the view returns a redirect to a GET URL instead of rendering a page directly. The browser then loads that page with GET, so refreshing it does not resubmit the form and create a duplicate record.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0026","card":"q-flask-008-the-three-project-routes-app-py","sec":"flask-s009","q":"In Flask, what is the difference between <code>url_for(\"dashboard\")</code> and <code>render_template(\"dashboard.html\")</code>?","a":"<p><code>url_for</code> takes a view function (endpoint) name and builds the URL for it. It is used in <code>redirect(...)</code> and in templates for links. <code>render_template</code> takes a template filename and returns rendered HTML. Mixing them up, such as <code>url_for(\"dashboard.html\")</code>, raises a <code>BuildError</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0027","card":"q-flask-008-the-three-project-routes-app-py","sec":"flask-s009","q":"Why prefer <code>url_for</code> over hard-coding URLs like <code>\"/dashboard\"</code> in Flask?","a":"<p>If the route path changes, every <code>url_for</code> call still works because it looks up the endpoint. It also handles URL parameters, escaping, and a mounted application prefix. Hard-coded paths break silently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0028","card":"q-flask-008-the-three-project-routes-app-py","sec":"flask-s009","q":"What does the Flask <code>request</code> object give you?","a":"<p>Access to the incoming request: <code>request.method</code>, <code>request.form</code> (POST form data), <code>request.args</code> (query string), <code>request.headers</code>, <code>request.json</code>, <code>request.files</code> and <code>request.cookies</code>. It is a context-local proxy that is valid only while a request is being handled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0029","card":"q-flask-009-4-jinja-templating-and-inheritance","sec":"flask-s010","q":"In Jinja, what is the difference between <code>{{ ... }}</code> and <code>{% ... %}</code>?","a":"<p><code>{{ ... }}</code> is an expression: it evaluates and prints a value. <code>{% ... %}</code> is a statement: it runs logic such as <code>for</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>extends</code> and <code>block</code> and prints nothing itself. There is also <code>{# ... #}</code> for comments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0030","card":"q-flask-009-4-jinja-templating-and-inheritance","sec":"flask-s010","q":"Does Jinja in Flask escape variables by default, and why does that matter?","a":"<p>Yes, for templates ending in <code>.html</code>, <code>.htm</code>, <code>.xml</code> and <code>.xhtml</code>, Flask turns on autoescaping. A value like <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> is rendered as text, which protects against XSS. Use the <code>|safe</code> filter or <code>Markup</code> only for content you fully trust.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0031","card":"q-flask-010-passing-data-to-a-template","sec":"flask-s011","q":"In Flask, how do you make Python data available in a template?","a":"<p>Pass keyword arguments to <code>render_template</code>, for example <code>render_template(\"index.html\", title=\"Home\", user=user)</code>. Each keyword becomes a variable in the template, so <code>{{ title }}</code> and <code>{{ user.username }}</code> work. Dictionary keys and object attributes can both be read with dot notation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0032","card":"q-flask-010-passing-data-to-a-template","sec":"flask-s011","q":"In Jinja, what happens if you reference a variable that was not passed to the template?","a":"<p>By default Jinja uses an <code>Undefined</code> value that renders as an empty string, so nothing is printed and no error is raised. Using an attribute of it (<code>{{ missing.name }}</code>) raises an <code>UndefinedError</code>. This is why a typo in a variable name can look like blank output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0033","card":"q-flask-011-loops-conditionals-filters","sec":"flask-s012","q":"Given <code>{{ name | upper }}</code> in Jinja, what is the <code>|</code> and what does it do?","a":"<p>The pipe applies a filter, a function that transforms the value inline. <code>upper</code> returns the string in uppercase. Filters can be chained, as in <code>{{ name | lower | capitalize }}</code>. Other common filters are <code>length</code>, <code>default</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>safe</code> and <code>tojson</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0034","card":"q-flask-011-loops-conditionals-filters","sec":"flask-s012","q":"In Jinja, how do you loop over a list and handle an empty list?","a":"<p>Use <code>{% for item in items %}...{% else %}...{% endfor %}</code>. The <code>else</code> block runs when the sequence is empty. Inside the loop, the <code>loop</code> variable gives <code>loop.index</code>, <code>loop.first</code> and <code>loop.last</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0035","card":"q-flask-011-loops-conditionals-filters","sec":"flask-s012","q":"How do you write a conditional in Jinja, and does it need a closing tag?","a":"<p>Use <code>{% if cond %}...{% elif other %}...{% else %}...{% endif %}</code>. Yes, every <code>if</code> and <code>for</code> block needs its <code>end</code> tag, unlike Python which relies on indentation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0036","card":"q-flask-011-loops-conditionals-filters","sec":"flask-s012","q":"Why should data-heavy logic such as filtering or sorting a large query be done in the view rather than in the Jinja template?","a":"<p>Templates are meant for presentation. Logic in the view is easier to test, can be pushed to the database query, and keeps templates simple. Use template filters and conditionals for display decisions only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0037","card":"q-flask-012-template-inheritance","sec":"flask-s013","q":"In Jinja, how does template inheritance work and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>A base template defines the shared layout (head, navigation, footer) with named <code>{% block %}</code> placeholders. Child templates start with <code>{% extends \"base.html\" %}</code> and fill in those blocks. You change the layout in one place and every page updates, instead of copying HTML into each page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0038","card":"q-flask-012-template-inheritance","sec":"flask-s013","q":"In a child Jinja template, what happens to content written outside any <code>{% block %}</code>?","a":"<p>In a child template that uses <code>extends</code>, anything outside blocks is not rendered, since only block contents replace the parent's blocks. All page content must live inside blocks the parent defines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0039","card":"q-flask-012-template-inheritance","sec":"flask-s013","q":"In a Jinja base template, how do you let a child template add to a block instead of replacing it?","a":"<p>Call <code>{{ super() }}</code> inside the child's block. It outputs the parent's block content, and you add your own before or after. A typical use is adding an extra stylesheet or script to a <code>head</code> block.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0040","card":"q-flask-012-template-inheritance","sec":"flask-s013","q":"In a Flask base template, why link CSS with <code>{{ url_for('static', filename='css/style.css') }}</code> rather than a plain <code>/static/css/style.css</code>?","a":"<p><code>url_for('static', ...)</code> builds the path from the app's static configuration, so it keeps working if the static URL path or an application prefix changes. It also avoids typos in the base path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0041","card":"q-flask-012-template-inheritance","sec":"flask-s013","q":"Where should a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag for a JavaScript library be placed in a base template, and how do you let pages add their own scripts?","a":"<p>Place shared library tags near the end of <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> (or with <code>defer</code>) so they do not block page rendering. Add an empty <code>{% block scripts %}{% endblock %}</code> after them so a child page can inject page-specific script that runs after the library has loaded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0042","card":"q-flask-013-5-forms-with-flask-wtf","sec":"flask-s014","q":"What does Flask-WTF add on top of WTForms?","a":"<p>WTForms provides form classes, fields and validators. Flask-WTF integrates it with Flask: <code>FlaskForm</code> reads <code>request.form</code> automatically, adds CSRF protection, and offers <code>validate_on_submit()</code>. You write the form as a Python class instead of hand-building HTML and checking input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0043","card":"q-flask-013-5-forms-with-flask-wtf","sec":"flask-s014","q":"What is CSRF, and how does Flask-WTF protect against it?","a":"<p>Cross-site request forgery is an attack where a malicious site makes a logged-in user's browser submit a request to your app. Flask-WTF embeds a secret, per-session token as a hidden field and rejects POST requests whose token is missing or invalid. A foreign site cannot read the token, so its forged request fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0044","card":"q-flask-013-5-forms-with-flask-wtf","sec":"flask-s014","q":"In Flask-WTF, what happens if <code>SECRET_KEY</code> is not set?","a":"<p>CSRF token generation fails and the form raises an error about the missing secret key (it needs it to sign the token). The key must be a long random value kept out of source control, read for example from an environment variable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0045","card":"q-flask-014-define-route-render","sec":"flask-s015","q":"In Flask-WTF, what does <code>form.validate_on_submit()</code> do?","a":"<p>It returns <code>True</code> only when the request method is POST (or PUT, PATCH, DELETE) and every field validator, including the CSRF check, passes. On a GET it returns <code>False</code>, so the view falls through to render the empty form. On a failed POST it also returns <code>False</code>, and the re-rendered form carries the error messages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0046","card":"q-flask-014-define-route-render","sec":"flask-s015","q":"In a Flask-WTF template, why is <code>{{ form.hidden_tag() }}</code> required inside the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>?","a":"<p>It renders the hidden fields, including the CSRF token. Without it the token is not submitted, so <code>validate_on_submit()</code> fails on every POST. The symptom is a form that always re-renders with a CSRF error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0047","card":"q-flask-014-define-route-render","sec":"flask-s015","q":"In this Flask-WTF template, what does <code>size=20</code> do?","a":"<p>Calling a field renders its HTML <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>, and keyword arguments become HTML attributes. This produces an input with <code>size=\"20\"</code>. The same mechanism sets <code>class</code>, <code>placeholder</code> and <code>id</code>, for example <code>form.name(class=\"form-control\")</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">HTML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{{ form.name(size=20) }}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"flask-f0048","card":"q-flask-014-define-route-render","sec":"flask-s015","q":"In Flask-WTF, how do you read a submitted value in the view?","a":"<p>After validation, use <code>form.&lt;field&gt;.data</code>, for example <code>form.email.data</code>. The value is already converted by the field type, so an <code>IntegerField</code> gives an <code>int</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0049","card":"q-flask-014-define-route-render","sec":"flask-s015","q":"In this Flask-WTF view, why is the form object created on every request and passed to the template?","a":"<p>The template needs the form object to render fields and, after a failed POST, to show the submitted values and the validation errors. On POST the <code>FlaskForm</code> fills itself from <code>request.form</code>. On GET it is empty.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@app.route(&quot;/form&quot;, methods=[&quot;GET&quot;, &quot;POST&quot;])\ndef form():\n    form = MyForm()\n    if form.validate_on_submit():\n        return redirect(url_for(&quot;done&quot;))\n    return render_template(&quot;form.html&quot;, form=form)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"flask-f0050","card":"q-flask-014-define-route-render","sec":"flask-s015","q":"In a Flask-WTF form template, how do you show validation errors next to a field?","a":"<p>Loop over the field's errors list: <code>{% for error in form.exercise.errors %}&lt;span&gt;{{ error }}&lt;/span&gt;{% endfor %}</code>. The list is empty before submission and after valid input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0051","card":"q-flask-015-common-validators","sec":"flask-s016","q":"In WTForms, what is the difference between <code>DataRequired</code> and <code>InputRequired</code>?","a":"<p><code>DataRequired</code> fails if the field's data is falsy, so empty strings, whitespace-only text and also the number <code>0</code> fail. <code>InputRequired</code> fails only if no input was submitted at all, so <code>0</code> passes. For numeric fields where zero is valid, use <code>InputRequired</code> with <code>NumberRange</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0052","card":"q-flask-015-common-validators","sec":"flask-s016","q":"In WTForms, what do <code>Email()</code> and <code>NumberRange(min, max)</code> validate, and is there a dependency to install?","a":"<p><code>Email()</code> checks the string looks like an email address. <code>NumberRange(min=0, max=24)</code> checks that a number lies within the bounds, and either bound is optional. In WTForms 3, <code>Email()</code> needs the separate <code>email_validator</code> package (<code>pip install email-validator</code>), otherwise it raises an error when used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0053","card":"q-flask-015-common-validators","sec":"flask-s016","q":"Why is server-side validation with WTForms still needed if the HTML input has <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> attributes?","a":"<p>Client-side attributes can be bypassed with browser tools or direct HTTP requests. The server must validate every input because it is the only trusted boundary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0054","card":"q-flask-016-project-health-data-form","sec":"flask-s017","q":"In WTForms, which field classes would you choose for a whole-number input and a calendar date, and why?","a":"<p><code>IntegerField</code> renders a number input and converts the submitted string to an <code>int</code>, rejecting non-numeric text. <code>DateField</code> parses a date string (default format <code>%Y-%m-%d</code>) to a <code>datetime.date</code>. Using typed fields means the view receives Python values that match the database column types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0055","card":"q-flask-016-project-health-data-form","sec":"flask-s017","q":"In WTForms, how would you limit a number of hours slept to between 0 and 24?","a":"<p>Add <code>NumberRange(min=0, max=24)</code> to the validators, usually together with <code>InputRequired()</code>, as in <code>IntegerField(\"Sleep (hours)\", validators=[InputRequired(), NumberRange(min=0, max=24)])</code>. Values outside the range produce an error message on the field.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0056","card":"q-flask-016-project-health-data-form","sec":"flask-s017","q":"In Flask-WTF, why does the view read <code>form.date.data</code> only inside <code>if form.validate_on_submit():</code>?","a":"<p>Before validation passes, the data may be missing or invalid, for example text in a number field. Reading it only in the valid branch guarantees correct types and ranges before it is stored.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0057","card":"q-flask-017-6-database-operations-with-flask-sqlalch","sec":"flask-s018","q":"What is an ORM, and what does Flask-SQLAlchemy give you?","a":"<p>An object-relational mapper lets you work with database tables as Python classes and rows as objects instead of writing raw SQL. Flask-SQLAlchemy integrates SQLAlchemy with Flask: it provides <code>db.Model</code>, <code>db.Column</code>, a request-scoped <code>db.session</code>, and configuration through <code>app.config</code>. The benefits are portability across databases and fewer SQL injection mistakes. The costs are an abstraction layer and sometimes inefficient queries to tune.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0058","card":"q-flask-017-6-database-operations-with-flask-sqlalch","sec":"flask-s018","q":"What is the SQLAlchemy session and why do changes need <code>commit()</code>?","a":"<p>The session is a workspace that tracks objects you have added, changed or deleted and holds them until you call <code>db.session.commit()</code>, which writes them in one transaction. If something fails, <code>db.session.rollback()</code> discards the pending changes. This keeps related changes atomic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0059","card":"q-flask-017-6-database-operations-with-flask-sqlalch","sec":"flask-s018","q":"What are the main alternatives to Flask-SQLAlchemy for storing data in Flask apps?","a":"<p>Plain SQLAlchemy without the extension, raw DB-API drivers such as <code>sqlite3</code> or <code>psycopg2</code> with hand-written SQL, or other ORMs like Peewee. Raw SQL gives full control and speed for simple cases. An ORM gives faster development and portability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0060","card":"q-flask-018-configuration-and-creating-the-database","sec":"flask-s019","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, what does the <code>SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI</code> setting contain?","a":"<p>A database URL that names the dialect and location, for example <code>sqlite:///app.db</code> for a local SQLite file or <code>postgresql://user:pass@host/dbname</code> for PostgreSQL. Changing this one value moves the app to another database, as long as the matching driver is installed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0061","card":"q-flask-018-configuration-and-creating-the-database","sec":"flask-s019","q":"What does <code>SQLALCHEMY_TRACK_MODIFICATIONS = False</code> do in Flask-SQLAlchemy?","a":"<p>It turns off the signal system that tracks every object change, which used extra memory and CPU. In Flask-SQLAlchemy 3.0 and later the default is already <code>False</code>, and the setting is rarely needed. In 2.x it defaulted to <code>None</code> with a warning, so apps set it to <code>False</code> explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0062","card":"q-flask-018-configuration-and-creating-the-database","sec":"flask-s019","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, what does <code>db.create_all()</code> do, and what does it not do?","a":"<p>It creates tables for all models that are imported and registered, if they do not already exist. It does not alter existing tables, so adding a column to a model does nothing to an existing table. For schema changes use migrations (Flask-Migrate with Alembic). In Flask-SQLAlchemy 3.x it must run inside an application context, for example <code>with app.app_context(): db.create_all()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0063","card":"q-flask-018-configuration-and-creating-the-database","sec":"flask-s019","q":"Why must a model be imported before calling <code>db.create_all()</code> in a Flask shell or script?","a":"<p><code>create_all</code> only knows about models whose classes have been defined, because defining a <code>db.Model</code> subclass registers its table in the metadata. If the module defining the model is never imported, no table is created for it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0064","card":"q-flask-018-configuration-and-creating-the-database","sec":"flask-s019","q":"What is <code>FLASK_APP</code> used for, and what is the modern alternative?","a":"<p>It tells the <code>flask</code> CLI which module holds the app, so <code>flask run</code> and <code>flask shell</code> work. In Flask 2.2 and later you can pass <code>--app app</code> instead, for example <code>flask --app app shell</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0065","card":"q-flask-018-configuration-and-creating-the-database","sec":"flask-s019","q":"Why is SQLite fine for development but usually replaced in production deployments?","a":"<p>SQLite is a single file with no server, which makes setup trivial. It has limited concurrent writes and no built-in network access. A server database such as PostgreSQL supports concurrency, backups and remote access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0066","card":"q-flask-019-models","sec":"flask-s020","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, how do you define a table?","a":"<p>Create a class that inherits from <code>db.Model</code> and give it <code>db.Column</code> attributes. Each attribute becomes a column, and the class becomes a table (default name is the snake-case class name). Each instance is a row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0067","card":"q-flask-019-models","sec":"flask-s020","q":"In a Flask-SQLAlchemy model, what do <code>primary_key=True</code>, <code>nullable=False</code> and <code>unique=True</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>primary_key=True</code> makes the column the unique row identifier (an integer one auto-increments). <code>nullable=False</code> forbids NULL, so a value is required. <code>unique=True</code> adds a uniqueness constraint, so inserting a duplicate raises an <code>IntegrityError</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0068","card":"q-flask-019-models","sec":"flask-s020","q":"In a Flask-SQLAlchemy model, why define <code>__repr__</code>?","a":"<p>It gives each object a readable string in the shell, logs and debugging output, such as <code>&lt;User alice&gt;</code>, instead of a memory address.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0069","card":"q-flask-019-models","sec":"flask-s020","q":"In <code>db.Column(db.String(80))</code>, what does the 80 do?","a":"<p>It sets the maximum length of the string column (<code>VARCHAR(80)</code>). SQLite does not enforce it, but PostgreSQL and MySQL do, so a longer value raises an error there. This is one way code that works on SQLite fails after moving to PostgreSQL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0070","card":"q-flask-020-crud-overview","sec":"flask-s021","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, what are the calls for Create, Read, Update and Delete?","a":"<p>Create: <code>db.session.add(obj)</code> then <code>commit()</code>. Read: <code>Model.query.all()</code> or a filtered query, no commit needed. Update: change an attribute on a loaded object, then <code>commit()</code>. Delete: <code>db.session.delete(obj)</code> then <code>commit()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0071","card":"q-flask-020-crud-overview","sec":"flask-s021","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, why does an update need no <code>db.session.add()</code>?","a":"<p>An object loaded through the session is already tracked. Changing an attribute marks it as modified, and <code>commit()</code> writes the change. <code>add</code> is only needed for new objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0072","card":"q-flask-020-crud-overview","sec":"flask-s021","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, what happens if you forget <code>db.session.commit()</code> after <code>add</code>?","a":"<p>The change stays pending in the session, and the transaction is rolled back at the end of the request, so the row never reaches the database. A common symptom is data that shows up within the same request but is gone on the next page load.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0073","card":"q-flask-021-create","sec":"flask-s022","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, what are the steps to save a submitted form as a new row?","a":"<p>Validate the form, build a model instance from the cleaned values (<code>User(username=form.name.data, ...)</code>), call <code>db.session.add(obj)</code>, call <code>db.session.commit()</code>, then redirect (POST-Redirect-GET).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0074","card":"q-flask-021-create","sec":"flask-s022","q":"In a Flask-SQLAlchemy model with <code>email = db.Column(db.String(120), unique=True)</code>, what happens when a form submits an email that already exists, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>The commit raises <code>sqlalchemy.exc.IntegrityError</code> (unique constraint failed). Handle it by checking first with <code>User.query.filter_by(email=...).first()</code>, or by catching the error, calling <code>db.session.rollback()</code> and showing a message. The pre-check can race under concurrency, so the constraint stays as the final guard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0075","card":"q-flask-021-create","sec":"flask-s022","q":"In Flask, what does <code>request.args.get(\"username\")</code> read, and why is it a weak way to pass data after a form submit?","a":"<p>It reads the query string, the part after <code>?</code> in the URL. Passing data this way puts it in the URL, browser history and server logs, and anyone can edit it. It is better to load the saved record from the database by ID, or to use <code>flash()</code> for a confirmation message.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0076","card":"q-flask-022-read","sec":"flask-s023","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, how do you fetch all rows, one row by primary key, and filtered rows?","a":"<p><code>Model.query.all()</code> gets all rows. <code>Model.query.get(id)</code> fetches by primary key. <code>Model.query.filter_by(email=\"a@b.com\").first()</code> or <code>.filter(...)</code> for conditions. In SQLAlchemy 2.0 style, <code>Model.query.get</code> is legacy and <code>db.session.get(Model, id)</code> is preferred, and the <code>db.session.execute(db.select(Model))</code> form replaces <code>query</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0077","card":"q-flask-022-read","sec":"flask-s023","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, what is the difference between <code>.first()</code>, <code>.get(id)</code> and <code>.first_or_404()</code>?","a":"<p><code>.first()</code> returns the first match or <code>None</code>. <code>.get(id)</code> looks up by primary key and returns <code>None</code> if missing. <code>.first_or_404()</code> (and <code>get_or_404</code>) aborts the request with a 404 response when nothing is found, which saves the manual check in a view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0078","card":"q-flask-022-read","sec":"flask-s023","q":"Why pass query results to <code>render_template</code> as a list or objects and loop in Jinja, instead of building HTML in the view?","a":"<p>The view stays focused on data access and the template on layout. Jinja can read model attributes directly (<code>{{ user.username }}</code>) and auto-escapes values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0079","card":"q-flask-023-update-and-delete","sec":"flask-s024","q":"In Flask, how does a form tell the server which record to update?","a":"<p>Include the record ID in the request, either as a hidden input (<code>&lt;input type=\"hidden\" name=\"user_id\" value=\"{{ user.id }}\"&gt;</code>) read with <code>request.form.get(\"user_id\")</code>, or in the URL (<code>/users/&lt;int:user_id&gt;/edit</code>). The view then loads the row by ID, changes fields and commits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0080","card":"q-flask-023-update-and-delete","sec":"flask-s024","q":"Why should a Flask delete route accept only POST rather than GET?","a":"<p>GET requests must be safe: browsers prefetch links, crawlers follow them, and links can be triggered from other sites. A delete behind GET can be fired accidentally or by CSRF. Use <code>methods=[\"POST\"]</code> (or DELETE from an API client) with a form button, ideally with a CSRF token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0081","card":"q-flask-023-update-and-delete","sec":"flask-s024","q":"In this Flask view, what is the pitfall if <code>user</code> is <code>None</code>?","a":"<p><code>db.session.delete(None)</code> raises an error (an <code>UnmappedInstanceError</code>) when the ID does not exist, producing a 500 response. Check <code>if user:</code> first or use <code>User.query.get_or_404(user_id)</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@app.route(&quot;/delete_user/&lt;int:user_id&gt;&quot;, methods=[&quot;POST&quot;])\ndef delete_user(user_id):\n    user = User.query.get(user_id)\n    db.session.delete(user)\n    db.session.commit()\n    return redirect(url_for(&quot;users&quot;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"flask-f0082","card":"q-flask-023-update-and-delete","sec":"flask-s024","q":"In Flask, what does the <code>action</code> attribute in <code>&lt;form method=\"POST\" action=\"{{ url_for('delete_user', user_id=user.id) }}\"&gt;</code> do?","a":"<p>It sets the URL the browser submits to. <code>url_for('delete_user', user_id=user.id)</code> builds <code>/delete_user/&lt;id&gt;</code> from the endpoint name, and an extra keyword fills the route's URL parameter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0083","card":"q-flask-024-project-healthdata-model-and-create-read","sec":"flask-s025","q":"In SQLAlchemy, which column type would you use for a calendar date, and what Python value must you assign?","a":"<p><code>db.Column(db.Date)</code> stores a date and needs a <code>datetime.date</code> object. Assigning a string such as <code>\"2024-01-01\"</code> raises an error on SQLite (it requires Python date objects) and is wrong on other databases too. A <code>DateField</code> in WTForms already returns a <code>date</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0084","card":"q-flask-024-project-healthdata-model-and-create-read","sec":"flask-s025","q":"In a Flask dashboard route, how do you retrieve all rows for a model and send them to the template?","a":"<p><code>rows = Model.query.all()</code> and then <code>render_template(\"dashboard.html\", data=rows)</code>. In the template, loop with <code>{% for entry in data %}</code> and print attributes with <code>{{ entry.field }}</code>. For large tables, filter, order and paginate in the query instead of loading everything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0085","card":"q-flask-024-project-healthdata-model-and-create-read","sec":"flask-s025","q":"In Flask-SQLAlchemy, how would you list the entries in date order, newest first?","a":"<p>Use <code>Model.query.order_by(Model.date.desc()).all()</code>. Ordering in the database is faster and more reliable than sorting in Python or the template.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0086","card":"q-flask-025-7-data-visualization-with-chart-js","sec":"flask-s026","q":"How do the pieces fit together when a Flask app draws a chart with Chart.js?","a":"<p>The view queries the database and builds plain Python lists (labels and values). The template passes them to JavaScript with the <code>tojson</code> filter. A <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> element in the page provides the drawing area, and <code>new Chart(ctx, config)</code> renders the chart from a config with <code>type</code>, <code>data</code> and <code>options</code>. Flask does the data work and the browser draws the chart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0087","card":"q-flask-025-7-data-visualization-with-chart-js","sec":"flask-s026","q":"Why include the Chart.js script in the base template rather than in each page?","a":"<p>Every page that extends the base gets the library, and you load it from one place, for example from a CDN. The trade-off is that pages without charts also download it, so a <code>{% block scripts %}</code> in pages that need it avoids that cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0088","card":"q-flask-025-7-data-visualization-with-chart-js","sec":"flask-s026","q":"What are the trade-offs of loading Chart.js from a CDN versus serving it from <code>static/</code>?","a":"<p>A CDN is fast to set up and often cached, but depends on a third party being available and requires a pinned version to avoid surprise changes. Serving it from <code>static/</code> removes that dependency, works offline and can be integrity-checked, but you host and update the file yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0089","card":"q-flask-026-seeding-demo-data","sec":"flask-s027","q":"In Python, why do you call <code>datetime.strptime(text, \"%Y-%m-%d\").date()</code> before saving a date to a <code>db.Date</code> column?","a":"<p><code>strptime</code> parses a string into a <code>datetime</code> using the format. <code>.date()</code> drops the time part to give a <code>datetime.date</code>, which is what a <code>Date</code> column expects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0090","card":"q-flask-026-seeding-demo-data","sec":"flask-s027","q":"What is the risk of seeding a database through a normal route such as <code>/add_data</code>?","a":"<p>Every visit inserts the rows again, so you get duplicates, and anyone who finds the URL can modify the data. Seed with a one-off script or a Flask CLI command, make it idempotent by checking for existing data, and never expose it in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0091","card":"q-flask-027-charting-database-data-single-chart","sec":"flask-s028","q":"In a Jinja template that embeds data in JavaScript, why use <code>{{ dates | tojson }}</code> and not <code>{{ dates }}</code>?","a":"<p><code>tojson</code> serializes the Python value as valid JSON, with correct quoting, <code>null</code> for <code>None</code> and escaping of characters that could end a script tag. Plain <code>{{ dates }}</code> prints the Python repr, such as single-quoted strings, which can break the JavaScript and is an XSS risk. <code>tojson</code> is how server data safely enters JS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0092","card":"q-flask-027-charting-database-data-single-chart","sec":"flask-s028","q":"In a Flask chart view, why convert date objects with <code>strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\")</code> before sending them to the template?","a":"<p>Chart labels should be short, predictable strings. Flask's JSON provider serializes a <code>datetime.date</code> in HTTP-date style (for example <code>Mon, 01 Jan 2024 00:00:00 GMT</code>), which is a poor axis label. Formatting with <code>strftime</code> gives the exact text you want and works with any JSON serializer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0093","card":"q-flask-027-charting-database-data-single-chart","sec":"flask-s028","q":"In Chart.js, what do <code>type</code>, <code>data.labels</code>, <code>data.datasets</code> and <code>options.scales.y.beginAtZero</code> control?","a":"<p><code>type</code> selects the chart kind (<code>bar</code>, <code>line</code>, <code>pie</code>). <code>labels</code> are the x-axis categories. <code>datasets</code> is an array of series, each with a <code>label</code>, <code>data</code> array and styling such as <code>backgroundColor</code> and <code>borderColor</code>. <code>beginAtZero: true</code> makes the y-axis start at zero, which avoids visually exaggerating differences in bar charts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0094","card":"q-flask-027-charting-database-data-single-chart","sec":"flask-s028","q":"In Chart.js, what is the difference between <code>getContext('2d')</code> and <code>new Chart(ctx, config)</code>?","a":"<p><code>getContext('2d')</code> returns the drawing context of the <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> element, the surface to paint on. <code>new Chart(ctx, config)</code> creates a chart bound to that context and draws it using the config. The canvas must exist in the DOM before this script runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0095","card":"q-flask-027-charting-database-data-single-chart","sec":"flask-s028","q":"In Chart.js, how do you change a bar chart into a line chart?","a":"<p>Change <code>type</code> from <code>'bar'</code> to <code>'line'</code>. For a line you usually drop <code>backgroundColor</code> or set <code>fill: false</code> and use <code>borderWidth</code> as the line thickness. The data and labels stay the same.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0096","card":"q-flask-027-charting-database-data-single-chart","sec":"flask-s028","q":"In a page that loads Chart.js, why does the chart script sit after the <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> element?","a":"<p>The script looks up the canvas with <code>document.getElementById</code>, which returns <code>null</code> if the element has not been parsed yet, and then <code>getContext</code> fails. Place the script after the canvas or run it in a <code>DOMContentLoaded</code> handler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0097","card":"q-flask-028-dashboard-with-three-charts","sec":"flask-s029","q":"How do you draw several Chart.js charts on one page?","a":"<p>Give each <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code> a unique <code>id</code>, get a context for each, and call <code>new Chart</code> once per canvas with its own config. The charts can share one labels array, such as dates, while each has its own dataset, label and color.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0098","card":"q-flask-028-dashboard-with-three-charts","sec":"flask-s029","q":"In Flask, how do you pass several data series from one view to a template?","a":"<p>Pass each as a keyword argument to <code>render_template</code>, for example <code>render_template(\"dashboard.html\", dates=dates, exercise=exercise, sleep=sleep)</code>, then in the template <code>const sleepData = {{ sleep | tojson }};</code>. Alternatively, build one dictionary and send it with a single <code>tojson</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0099","card":"q-flask-028-dashboard-with-three-charts","sec":"flask-s029","q":"What happens if two canvas elements on one page share the same <code>id</code>?","a":"<p><code>document.getElementById</code> returns only the first one, so one chart is drawn twice or into the wrong canvas and the other stays empty. HTML requires <code>id</code> values to be unique.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0100","card":"q-flask-029-8-deployment-to-vercel","sec":"flask-s030","q":"Why can't a Flask app on a serverless platform like Vercel rely on a local SQLite file?","a":"<p>Serverless functions run on a read-only or temporary filesystem, and each invocation may run in a fresh instance. Writes to a SQLite file would fail or vanish. Use an external managed database such as PostgreSQL and read its URL from an environment variable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0101","card":"q-flask-029-8-deployment-to-vercel","sec":"flask-s030","q":"In a Flask <code>requirements.txt</code> for a PostgreSQL deployment, what is <code>psycopg2-binary</code> for?","a":"<p>It is the PostgreSQL driver that SQLAlchemy uses to talk to the database. The <code>-binary</code> build ships precompiled, so installation does not need PostgreSQL headers or a compiler. The psycopg project recommends compiling from source for production, but the binary package is common for quick deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0102","card":"q-flask-029-8-deployment-to-vercel","sec":"flask-s030","q":"In a Flask config, what does <code>os.environ.get(\"SECRET_KEY\", \"your-secret-key\")</code> do, and what is the risk of the fallback?","a":"<p>It reads the value from the environment, and uses the second argument if the variable is missing. The risk is that a forgotten variable in production silently falls back to a publicly known key, which lets attackers forge session cookies and CSRF tokens. In production, fail loudly if the variable is missing, and keep real secrets out of the code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0103","card":"q-flask-029-8-deployment-to-vercel","sec":"flask-s030","q":"In a Flask config for deployment, how do you use SQLite locally and PostgreSQL in production without changing code?","a":"<p>Read the database URL from an environment variable with a local default: <code>os.environ.get(\"POSTGRES_URL\", \"sqlite:///app.db\")</code>. Production sets the variable and local development does not. Note that SQLAlchemy 1.4 and later rejects URLs beginning with <code>postgres://</code>, so a provider URL may need to be changed to <code>postgresql://</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0104","card":"q-flask-029-8-deployment-to-vercel","sec":"flask-s030","q":"In Flask, what does <code>@app.before_first_request</code> do, and is it still available?","a":"<p>It registered a function that ran once before the first request, often used to call <code>db.create_all()</code>. It was deprecated in Flask 2.2 and removed in Flask 2.3. In current versions, run <code>db.create_all()</code> inside <code>with app.app_context():</code> at startup, or use migrations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0105","card":"q-flask-029-8-deployment-to-vercel","sec":"flask-s030","q":"What does this Vercel configuration do?","a":"<p><code>builds</code> tells Vercel to build <code>app.py</code> with the Python runtime (<code>@vercel/python</code>) as a serverless function. <code>routes</code> sends every path, matched by the regex <code>/(.*)</code>, to that function so Flask handles routing. This is the legacy <code>builds</code>/<code>routes</code> configuration. Vercel's current docs use a different layout, such as an <code>app.py</code> entrypoint detected automatically, so check the present documentation.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n    &quot;version&quot;: 2,\n    &quot;builds&quot;: [{ &quot;src&quot;: &quot;app.py&quot;, &quot;use&quot;: &quot;@vercel/python&quot; }],\n    &quot;routes&quot;: [{ &quot;src&quot;: &quot;/(.*)&quot;, &quot;dest&quot;: &quot;app.py&quot; }]\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"flask-f0106","card":"q-flask-029-8-deployment-to-vercel","sec":"flask-s030","q":"Why must you redeploy after adding environment variables on a hosting platform like Vercel?","a":"<p>Environment variables are applied when a deployment is built or started. An already running deployment keeps its old values, so a new deployment is needed for it to see the new variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0107","card":"q-flask-030-deploying-with-the-vercel-cli","sec":"flask-s031","q":"In the Vercel CLI, what is the difference between <code>vercel</code> and <code>vercel --prod</code>?","a":"<p><code>vercel</code> creates a preview deployment at a unique URL so you can test changes. <code>vercel --prod</code> deploys to the production domain. The usual flow is to test on a preview and then promote to production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0108","card":"q-flask-030-deploying-with-the-vercel-cli","sec":"flask-s031","q":"What are the steps to deploy a Flask app with a PostgreSQL database on Vercel?","a":"<p>Make sure <code>requirements.txt</code> and the Vercel config are present. Run <code>vercel login</code> and <code>vercel</code> to create the project. Create a Postgres database in the project's Storage settings and connect it, which sets the connection environment variables. Add <code>SECRET_KEY</code> as an environment variable. Redeploy so the variables apply, test the preview URL, then run <code>vercel --prod</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"flask-f0109","card":"q-flask-030-deploying-with-the-vercel-cli","sec":"flask-s031","q":"After connecting a new Postgres database to a deployed Flask app, why might pages still fail until tables are created?","a":"<p>A new database is empty, with no tables and no data from local SQLite. The app must create the schema, for example with <code>db.create_all()</code> at startup or a migration run, before queries work.</p>","hand":true}];
